A||N||7||saleenfiero||Uh oh, unidentified noise||||10||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||09-14-2000||06:01 PM||||Recently, I've had this problem with my car making some kind of clicking or chafing noise when I make a right turn.  It seems to go faster in accordance with the speed of the wheels.  Any ideas on what this might be?||||reg||10
Z||000001||StuGood||09-14-2000||06:13 PM||||First check to see if anything is rubbing / clicking against the wheel+tire. Raise and support the front end -then rotate tire by hand.  You might find what it is.<P>Brake hardware is next guess - remove wheel + tire and rotate the rotor.  All this is to see if it's something obvious/ visible.<P>Next guess (going deeper) is wheel bearings.  If one side needs replacing, go ahead and do them both.<P>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !||||reg||4
Z||000002||stsmithgt||09-14-2000||06:55 PM||||Yeah, good luck.  I've got a similar noise.  Mines been in the shop all day and they said they can't identify what's making the noise.  I suppose it's a problem hard to duplicate up on a rack and it's probably not a bad enough problem to see it visually.  I have a feeling they're going to try to bend me over.  That seems to be the theme here in Monterey.  Good luck finding a solution. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000003||theogre||09-14-2000||10:23 PM||||Check the sheilds on the brake rotors. take each wheel off and look at the sheilds for scuff marks where they've hit the rotors.<P>Check front and back.<P>The rear wheel bearing has a certain amount of lateral movement built into it. It will allow the rotor to contact the shield if the sheild is even a little bent out of place.||||reg||1
Z||000004||saleenfiero||09-15-2000||01:23 PM||||Oh I guess I forgot to say that I'm sure it was coming from the back.  It was also making a little rattling noise when the car was in park and the wheel was cranked a little to the right.  Haven't had time to check yet for obstructions.  Thaanx for all the help guys.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ur2biteme||09-16-2000||12:11 AM||||i want to say possibly c.v. joints.  any one else?<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>169k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000006||Homicidal||09-16-2000||03:29 AM||||CV's for me.  Had that problem with my Audi.  B!tch to replace those things on an Audi, not sure on a Fiero, I assume it's the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-16-2000||12:38 PM||||Could be an axle but the shields should be checked first. Remember that Fiero axles have a far smaller range of motions than FWD axles get. When they tick in Fiero they do it pretty much all the time. not just turning.<P>replacing a Fiero axle takes less than an hour per side. take off the axle nut, Undo the ball joint, pull out the axle. (Don't forget to replace the seal in the tranny while the axle is out. Make sure you don't scar the seal hole prying it out.)<P>Getting the axle nut off, and final torque, is easiest with the wheel on for most people. (Use the spare tire if you have AL wheels and don't want to mess with center caps.) With the wheel on and the car on the ground you can get a much better effect out of the wrench since the car and suspension won't bounce.||||reg||1
